# Diagnosis report

## Data
- K: 222x222 square matrix; singular values s_1 = 1.000000 ... s_222 = 1.744e-15;
  condition number = 5.735e+14.
- Spectrum decays geometrically: per-index ratio r = 0.85746
  (log-linear fit over all 222 singular values, essentially exact).
- y: 222-vector, ||y|| = 1.97962.

## Fourier (Picard) coefficients
yhat_i = u_i^T y (left-singular basis), written to picard_data.csv with the
signed coefficient and picard_ratio = |yhat_i| / s_i.

- The coefficients do NOT decay: they plateau at the noise level for i >~ 40
  (median |yhat_i| over i > 50 = 0.01887; expected 0.798*sigma
  = 0.02304 for white Gaussian noise).
- The Picard ratio therefore diverges like sigma/s_i in the tail (max ratio
  2.159e+13 at i = 222). The divergence is driven by the
  coefficients flattening at the noise floor while s_i -> 0, so the divergence
  rate independently re-estimates the noise level:
  sigma ~= (median-tail |yhat_i|) * sqrt(pi/2) = 0.02365.

## Noise estimate and structure
sigma_hat = sqrt(mean(yhat_i^2, i > 50)) = 0.028875
(robust MAD cross-check: 0.027978).

Whiteness checks (noise appears white Gaussian; no whitening needed):
- Tail powers yhat_i^2/sigma^2 quantiles [0.015 0.108 0.427 1.469 4.059] vs chi2(1) theory [0.004 0.102 0.455 1.323 3.841];
  KS test vs N(0, sigma_hat^2): p = 0.113.
- Standard-basis autocorrelation of y: max |rho_lag| = 0.130 (lags 1..50,
  SE ~= 0.067) -> no significant color.
- Periodogram of y: largest peak / median = 6.1 -> no dominant frequency.

noise_estimate: 0.028875

## Effective rank
effective_rank = #{s_i > sigma_hat} = 24  (s_24 = 0.02910 > sigma_hat,
s_25 = 0.02496 < sigma_hat).
Sensitivity: at a 2*sigma_hat threshold the count is 19.
In the head (i <= k_eff), the 99%-significant Fourier coefficients
(yhat_i^2 > 6.635*sigma^2) are at 1-based indices [1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 9, 10, 13, 16, 18, 20, 22, 26]; the detectable
signal (information level) extends over roughly the first
27 singular directions
(95.8% of the data power lies in the first 26).
The 4 exceedances in the far tail (i > 26) are consistent with
chance fluctuations of the noise (expected count ~ 2.0 out of
196 tail samples at the 99% level).

effective_rank: 24

## Discrepancy / Picard judgment
The Picard (discrepancy) condition for y to lie in Range(K) requires
yhat_i/s_i -> 0. Here |yhat_i| plateaus at ~ sigma_hat and s_i -> 0, so the
ratio diverges: the observed data are not in Range(K), consistent with
y = K x_true + noise. Regularization is required.

dpc_judgment: does_not_hold

## Regime
sigma_hat*sqrt(n) = 0.43022 vs ||y|| = 1.97962:
the data are signal-dominated in the head (first ~24 directions) with a
clean white-noise floor in the tail. Standard parameter-selection rules apply
directly (the noise level is known from the data).
